  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale  \n   Voronoi diagrams are promising for constructing step functions by partitioning the interval [0, 2] into smaller segments. By adjusting the diagram's vertices (which are stored as a compression), we can explore many configurations at a step in the optimization. The aim is to minimize the maximum overlap integral \\( C_5 \\) by ensuring that the Voronoi vertices are placed to reduce instances of large overlaps between \\( h(x) \\) and \\( (1 - h(x+k)) \\). The initial parent construction (with 95 samples) will guide the placement of vertices, and the Voronoi representation simplifies storing and optimizing the step function.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification  \n   Let \\( \\mathcal{V} \\) be the set of Voronoi vertices, and let \\( \\mathcal{P} \\) be the set of power vertices representing the reversible events in the partition. The total number of Voronoi edges is related to \\( |\\mathcal{V}| \\). We represent the optimization variables \\( h \\) as a compression of \\( \\mathcal{V} \\), normalized to satisfy \\( \\sum h = n_{\\text{points}}/2 \\) (ensuring the integral condition). The cost function is \\( C_5 = \\max_k \\int h(x) (1 - h(x+k)) dx \\), computed as the maximum cross-correlation value scaled by \\( dx \\). The active set of constraints is based on power ratios between Voronoi events.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure  \n   For given \\( n \\text{ (n_points)} \\):  \n   - If the initial parent (or fallback) is available, use it to seed Voronoi simulations.  \n   - In `run`, start by selecting \\( n\\_points = 100, 200, 400 \\) if possible, but start with \\( 95 \\) (the parent size).  \n   - Use `voronoi_optimize` (defined later) with initial seed from `initial_h_values` and box-constraint bounds.  \n   - The `evaluate` function computes \\( C_5 \\) directly via Voronoi simulation.  \n   - During optimization:  \n     1. Transform parent samples to Voronoi vertices.  \n     2. Use SLSQP with analytical constraints via `event_vector` and `transition_power`.  \n     3. Clip and project back to power-compatible values.  \n   - Return the best \\( h \\) found (along with \\( C_5 \\) and \\( n\\_points \\)).\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations  \n   - Multi-start from 5 perturbations (each with minor rounding of power events).  \n   - Local high-fidelity optimization with Kalman-integrated initializations.  \n   - Fitness evaluation via `vaf_validate` and adaptive projection (using `decompose_events`).\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness  \n   Run 5 optimization trials per \\( n\\_points \\) (100, 200, 400) in order. Each trial is bounded by the total budget, with total wall time < 1100 seconds. After compute failure, use the last known valid solution.  \n   Fallback: Parent's \\( 95 \\)-sample solution if generation/evaluation fails.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract  \n   - After optimization, validate \\( h \\) for \\( [0,1] \\) entries and integral constraint.  \n   - Check `is_valid` before registering the best candidate.  \n   - Run fallback if candidate is invalid.",
